Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:12.720 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  Netflix's horror series the haunting of  Hill House earned critical acclaim for  its first season it's a modern  reimagining of the classic novel by  Shirley Jackson but the highlight of the  show is the two storms episode the sixth  one in the season the fifty-three minute  episode looks like it was filmed in one  long continuous shot seamlessly  transitioning between parallel  storylines in the past and present the  effect is seriously unsettling but how  exactly did the cast and crew pull this  off first series creator Mike Flanagan  made an ambitious play a film the bulk  of the episode in just five unedited  takes with a centerpiece tracking shot  that last 17 minutes long for this  challenge  Flanagan joined forces with Michael fimo  yari the cinematographer behind another  Netflix hit to all the boys I've loved  before Hill House was their seventh  project together following a string of  hit horror films but this one episode  presented the biggest challenge of their  careers to date the action unfolds in  two settings a tense family reunion at  the cranes funeral home in the present  and a stormy night they spent at Hill  House in the past even in the early  phases of production both the funeral  home and Hill House were designed with  the needs of episode six in mind the  stages had to be adjacent and connected  by a hallway so that Hugh Crain could  walk from the funeral home directly into  his past the sets also had to include  hiding places for crew equipment and  special lighting rigs along with secret  access portals for the actors and their  photo doubles to sprint through as the  scenes slit between past and present  once the set pieces were built  production was shut down for a month so  that they could focus on rehearsing the  episode with second team stand-ins  they'd walk through the and  script over and over and over again so  that the crew could learn all the  blocking lighting and camera  choreography by heart it was a dance  that they rehearsed and rehearsed and  rehearsed this also gave him a chance to  figure out the hundreds of camera and  lighting cues which would serve as  signals for the cast and crew to carry  out specific actions at the correct  times those actions could include  anything from a special effect to a set  movement a lighting change or a camera  pivot after the first cues were  important for getting the beauty  lighting right - if a cue was earlier  late or a cast member missed their mark  then the actors wouldn't be lit properly  once a minute that goes bad you have to  start over so there's a lot of pressure  and a lot of excitement the third shot  was the hardest it was the longest it  represented the most physical challenges  the following shot was only about a  third of the lane but it presented the  most technical difficulty as the crew  basically had to teleport Olivia around  the set in terms of coordination  physical coordination it was a challenge  it required a lot of handoffs we had  doubles for Olivia that were flying in  and out of rooms they even built a  handmade elevator for shot 4 which  lowered the cameraman down to the first  floor of Hill House as Olivia and Hugh  ran down the stairs  the weather effects added yet another  layer of complexity episode 6 is set  against the backdrop of an ongoing rain  storm which is occurring in both the  present and past Hill house relied  mostly on practical wizardry for its  special effects and creating the storm  was no different they brought in  specialty lights to create the Lightning  and installed massive rain effects which  actually flooded the set a few times  during rehearsal we thought it was  important that you could feel the water  effects see the one section that was was  CG was where all the windows blow since  there would be very little editing done  in post-production the actors had no  room for error at all if one person made  a minor misstep or jumbled their lines  they all have to start over our young  cast they were incredible we didn't  change the way we do things for them  based on their age and so episode 6 was  no different they were part of the team  like everybody else this massive  undertaking was well worth it in the end  the single shot effect in Episode six  sucks you into the action subverting  both space and time  critics called it a standout episode in  a series that's gotten props from horror  auteur Stephen King we were exploring  ways that we could bring memory into the  present and blur those lines whether  that's something that's actually  occurring in a supernatural way or  something that's only happening in the  mind Hillhouse brought that to another  level to be able to look away from that  may reduced its intensity  it's not something we do every day but  it was right it was right for that  episode  [Music]

